			"Message": "refactor(Output): Replace hardcoded drag selectors with stylesheet-driven extraction\n\nRather than maintaining a brittle hardcoded list of titlebar drag and no-drag selectors in the polyfill itself, walk every accessible stylesheet rule at runtime to discover which elements VS Code already declares as `-webkit-app-region: drag` / `no-drag` and stamp the equivalent `data-tauri-drag-region` attribute.\n\n`Walk()` recurses into CSSMediaRule and CSSSupportsRule containers so OS-scoped and feature-gated rules (e.g. `@media (-webkit-min-device-pixel-ratio...)`) are picked up automatically. Selectors are cached after the first full pass and reused across MutationObserver callbacks; a second collection runs on `window.onload` to cover lazy-loaded extension stylesheets. Cross-origin sheets that throw on `cssRules` access are skipped defensively, and selector strings that fail `querySelectorAll` (e.g. `:has()` / `:where()` on older WebKit builds) are caught and skipped per-selector rather than aborting the pass.\n\nThis eliminates the need to manually update the polyfill whenever VS Code upstream changes its titlebar or sidebar drag-region markup — the polyfill now reflects whatever the stylesheets declare.\n\nMinor companion formatting: add blank-line separators in Editor and Terminal GPU layer CSS files for readability consistency with adjacent rule blocks.",
			"SHA": "510588e3948874783f2e60f2f79ec8559f846e1d"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-26T14:02:02Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				757,
				5
			],
			"Message": "feat(Output): Implement Tauri 2 drag region polyfill and macOS fullscreen detector\n\nTauri 2's overlay-titlebar window-drag hit-test ignores Chromium's `-webkit-app-region: drag` CSS rule used by stock VS Code; it requires the explicit `data-tauri-drag-region` HTML attribute on draggable elements. Without it, dragging the workbench titlebar selects text instead of moving the window.\n\nAdd the `InjectTauriDragRegion` polyfill and transform:\n- Stamps `data-tauri-drag-region` on `.titlebar-drag-region` and related selectors\n- Stamps `data-tauri-drag-region=\"false\"` on interactive children (menubar, command-center, window controls) so click events land correctly\n- Uses a `MutationObserver` on `<body>` to catch late-mounted drag regions (titlebar repaints on profile switch / window-mode toggle)\n- Transform injects the polyfill into `vs/code/electron-browser/workbench/workbench.js`, always active (cross-OS structural fix)\n\nExtend the MacTitlebarOffsetCSS polyfill with a fullscreen detector:\n- Triples-signal detection: HTML5 fullscreen API, `display-mode: fullscreen` media query, window-vs-screen dimension heuristic\n- Toggles `body.land-fullscreen` to reclaim the 80px left padding when macOS native fullscreen hides the traffic-light cluster\n- Asserts `padding-right: 0` explicitly so future stock CSS cannot introduce right-edge drift\n- The fullscreen detector and stylesheet are installed together on DOMContentLoaded\n\nGate `InjectPartZIndexCSS` behind `DisableUIFixes`: the `isolation: isolate` rule this transform emits has been observed to hide the in-window menubar under specific configurations. Operators running with the env var set (e.g. when diagnosing menubar / dropdown clipping) now bypass this transform, returning workbench parts to stock CSS stacking-context behaviour.\n\nAll additions are idempotent (runtime markers prevent re-application). Existing comments updated to reflect always-active vs gated semantics.",
			"SHA": "9d76c4fb921c8bdc999fdadf8c0aaf48d26eebfd"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
